course: dictionary
lesson: 13512
title: Formal words: impart
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Impart is a formal word. It means to give or pass something on to someone, such as knowledge or news. «The teacher imparted her knowledge of rivers to the class.» The teacher gave the class facts about rivers, so impart means to pass on information.
explain: Impart can also mean to give a taste, smell or feeling to something. «Mint and lemon impart a fresh flavour to the tea.» The mint and lemon do not hand anything over to the tea. They add their flavour to it.
explain: You will meet impart in formal writing and exam questions. In everyday talk, you could use simpler words such as give, pass on or teach. Impart sounds more formal, and it is often used with knowledge, wisdom or news.
fact: Impart is a verb: impart, imparts, imparted, imparting.
fact: «Impart» means to give or pass on information, knowledge or wisdom to someone.
fact: «Impart» can also mean to give a quality, taste or feeling to something, as in «herbs impart flavour».
fact: Common phrases are impart knowledge, impart wisdom and impart news.
fact: Impart is a formal word, so you will find it mostly in formal writing, exams and cooking.
exercise: «The grandmother imparted her wisdom to the children.» What does imparted mean? | Forgot | Hid away | Broke | *Passed on
exercise: «Cinnamon imparts a warm flavour to apple pie.» What does imparts mean here? | Takes the taste away from | *Adds a taste or feeling to | Weighs | Cuts into small pieces
exercise: Which phrase means passing knowledge on to someone? | Hide knowledge | Lose knowledge | Eat knowledge | *impart knowledge
test: «The head teacher will impart the news at assembly.» What does impart mean? | Forget | Hide | *Give or pass on | Argue about
test: Which word could replace impart in «The guide imparted the history of the castle»? | Lost | Hid | *Shared | Broke
exam: Which sentence uses impart correctly? | *Herbs impart a strong flavour to the soup. | He imparted his bag to the shop. | We imparted the ball across the park. | The dog imparted loudly at the postman.
